/-
# Doubling $50 at roulette

On an American wheel, one $50 bet on red gives you a 47.4% chance of leaving
with $100. Bet $1 on red until you reach $100 or go broke, and the chance is
0.51%.

To run it locally, use `haze bold_play.hz`. The $1 strategy fails the last
section's 40% requirement, so the checker exits with status 1.
-/

/-
## What the smaller bets cost

A red followed by a black leaves the $1 player back at $50. That's two dollars
wagered even though the balance hasn't changed. There's no fifty-spin limit:
a player who wins a dollar can bet it again.

At a dollar a spin, the number of spins is also the number of dollars wagered.
This line in the [Haze model](#workbench) counts until the player reaches
either balance:

```haze
#expect Flat(18/38, 1, 50) |= steps until money == 0 or money == 100
```

The answer is 940.258 spins. Red pays even money on 18 pockets and loses on
the other 20, so each dollar bet costs 2/38 of a dollar on average. On about
$940 in wagers, the expected loss is $49.49. On one $50 bet, it's $2.63.
-/

system Flat(p : Num, stake : Num, start : Num) {
  var money : Num := start
  step {
    if money > 0 and money < 100 {
      money := money + (if flip(p) then stake else -stake)
    }
  }
}

#expect Flat(18/38, 1, 50) |= steps until money == 0 or money == 100
#prob Flat(18/38, 1, 50) |= eventually money == 100

/-
## The stake at $60

At $60, a $40 win is enough to reach $100. Betting the other $20 as well
would buy nothing toward that goal. Lose the $40 bet and you can still play.
The rule in the model is just:

```haze
let stake := min(money, 100 - money)
```

Dubins and Savage called this *bold play*. In *How to Gamble If You Must*
(1965), they proved it gives the best chance of reaching a fixed target in
an unfavorable even-money game with independent bets and no table limit.
It still loses money on average. Someone who wants to keep as much of the
$50 as possible can simply walk away. For a study of why a fixed target can
matter more than the expected payout, see [*Gambling for Goals*](https://www.nber.org/papers/w36010),
the household finance paper [discussed at Marginal Revolution](https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/11/gambling-for-goals.html).

![Probability of reaching $100 by starting balance. At $50 the bold strategy is at 47.4% and dollar bets are at 0.51%.](https://haze-lang.pages.dev/blog/images/bold-play-curve.svg)
The dashed line is a fair game. Both solid lines use the same American wheel.
-/

system Bold(p : Num, start : Num) {
  var money : Num := start
  step {
    if money > 0 and money < 100 {
      let stake := min(money, 100 - money)
      money := money + (if flip(p) then stake else -stake)
    }
  }
}

#prob Bold(18/38, 50) |= eventually money == 100
#prob Flat(18/38, 5, 50) |= eventually money == 100
#prob Flat(18/38, 10, 50) |= eventually money == 100
#prob Flat(18/38, 25, 50) |= eventually money == 100

/-
## One zero instead of two

On a European wheel there's no double zero. Suppose a single zero still takes
the full stake. The one-spin player's chance of doubling goes from 18/38 to
18/37, a gain of a little over one percentage point. For the $1 player it goes
from 0.51% to 6.28%. Removing one losing pocket makes that player more than
twelve times as likely to reach $100.

There's a short way to check those numbers. From halfway to the target, the
gambler's ruin formula for $1 stakes simplifies to `1 / (1 + (q/p)^50)`,
where `p` is the chance of winning a spin and `q` the chance of losing.
The exponent is fifty because you need fifty *more wins than losses*.
Use `20/18` for `q/p` on the American wheel and `19/18` on the European one.
-/

#prob Bold(18/37, 50) |= eventually money == 100
#prob Flat(18/37, 1, 50) |= eventually money == 100

/-
## Starting with $30

Bold play doesn't always end quickly. Start at $30 and win the first spin.
From $60, a loss, two wins and another loss take the bankroll through $20, $40
and $80 and straight back to $60. That loop can repeat. The `eventually` query
includes all those trips around the loop: Haze solves an equation for each
reachable balance. On the American wheel the chance of reaching $100 from $30
is 26.75% with bold play and 0.06% with $1 bets.

On a fair wheel both strategies give exactly 30%. There's a useful check:
the final balance must be either $0 or $100 and still average $30.

Subtract the bold player's chance from that fair-game line and the notches
are easier to see. At $50 one spin settles the game. From $25 a win lands
on $50; from $75 a loss lands there. More generally, if your balance is a
fraction `x` of the goal, the spin that keeps you in the game leaves `2x` or
`2x - 1`. Either operation drops the first binary digit after the point.
That's why smaller copies of the notch appear at eighths and sixteenths.
For arbitrarily fine stakes, Patrick Billingsley [shows](https://www.jstor.org/stable/27852139)
that the success curve is increasing and continuous but has derivative zero
almost everywhere.

![Bold play's shortfall from a fair wheel, in percentage points, for starting balances from $0 to $100.](https://haze-lang.pages.dev/blog/images/bold-play-shortfall.svg)
On this grid of 4,097 starting balances, the largest shortfall is 3.57 percentage points, near $66.67.
-/

#prob Bold(18/38, 30) |= eventually money == 100
#prob Flat(18/38, 1, 30) |= eventually money == 100
#expect Bold(18/38, 30) |= steps until money == 0 or money == 100
#prob Bold(1/2, 30) |= eventually money == 100
#prob Flat(1/2, 1, 30) |= eventually money == 100

/-
## If the odds are uncertain

Roulette has the convenient property that you can count the pockets. Suppose
instead that the bet still pays even money but you have to estimate the win
rate. You think 49% is the best estimate, though you wouldn't rule out 50.5%.
Here are four possible rates for the same game, each held fixed for a session:

```haze
param win : Num ~= {0.49: 1, 0.495: 0.7, 0.5: 0.4, 0.505: 0.2}
```

The weights after the colons describe how much support you give each estimate.
They aren't frequencies and needn't add to one. Here's the chance of turning
$50 into $100 at each rate:

| Chance of winning a spin | Betting $1 at a time | Betting $50 once |
|---:|---:|---:|
| 49% | 11.9% | 49% |
| 49.5% | 26.9% | 49.5% |
| 50% | 50% | 50% |
| 50.5% | 73.1% | 50.5% |

Say you want at least a 40% chance of doubling before you agree to play.
Haze checks that bound against [*belief*](https://haze-lang.pages.dev/docs/fuzzy/):
it takes the worst result at each level of support, then weights those results.
Here the worst rate for $1 bets is also the best-supported one, so belief is
11.9%. For the single bet it's 49%, enough to pass.

[Swap the weights on 49% and 50.5%](#workbench) and run it again. The percentages
in the table stay put. Even with the favorable rate ranked first, belief for
the $1 player only rises to 37.76%. The 49.5% estimate still carries weight
0.7, and at that rate the $1 player doubles up only about one time in four.
-/

param win : Num ~= {0.49: 1, 0.495: 0.7, 0.5: 0.4, 0.505: 0.2}

claim flat_doubles : Flat(win, 1, 50) |= eventually money == 100 with prob >= 0.4
claim bold_doubles : Bold(win, 50) |= eventually money == 100 with prob >= 0.4
